use is "The patient was referred to the specialist," and the reason given is in the relative clause starting with "because." In relative clauses, the antecedent is usually the nearest noun before the relative pronoun. Wait, but here the relative pronoun is "he," which is a pronoun, not a noun. That complicates things because pronouns don\'t act like typical relative pronouns like "who" or "which." \n\nSo maybe the rule is different here. Let\'s think about typical antecedent rules. When a sentence has a pronoun, we have to look backward in the sentence to find which noun it logically refers to. The sentence is structured as: The patient [was referred] to the specialist because [he] had a rare skin condition. The key here is that the patient was referred to the specialist for his own reason, which is having the condition. But the specialist might have the condition too? Wait, the sentence doesn\'t say the specialist had the condition, only that the patient had it because he was referred. \n\nWait, actually, the sentence structure is that the patient was referred because he had the condition. The problem is that "he" could refer to either the patient or the specialist. Let me parse the sentence again. The main subject is "the patient," and the specialist is the object of the verb "referred." The relative clause is the reason for the referral. The structure is: Because [Patient had a rare skin condition], they referred the patient to the specialist. Alternatively, maybe the specialist was referred because he had the condition? Wait, the sentence says "the patient was referred to the specialist because he had a rare skin condition." The "he" here is probably referring to the patient because the patient is the one who had the condition that necessitated the referral. The specialist\'s role is as the recipient of the referral, not the one with the condition. \n\nBut could there be ambiguity? Suppose someone reads it as the specialist had the condition, so they referred the patient to the specialist because the specialist had the condition. That would make less sense because usually, you refer a patient to a specialist because the patient needs help, not because the specialist has the condition. So the specialist\'s role is to treat the patient, not the other way around. Therefore, the more logical antecedent is "the patient." \n\nAlternatively, maybe in some interpretations, "he" could refer to the specialist if the sentence were structured differently. But as written, the sentence\'s structure places "the patient" as the subject of the main clause and the one with the condition. The specialist is the object. So "he" must refer back to "the patient." Therefore, the answer should be (A) The patient had a skin condition. \n\nWait but let me think again. Let\'s consider possible ambiguities. Suppose the sentence is: "The patient was referred to the specialist because he had a rare skin condition." If "he" is the pronoun, it could potentially refer to either the patient or the specialist. But in standard grammar, when a sentence is structured with a subject and then a reason in a relative clause starting with "because," the antecedent is the subject. So here, the subject is "the patient," so "he" would refer to the patient. \n\nAlternatively, if the sentence were structured as "The patient was referred to the specialist, who had a rare skin condition," then "who" would clearly refer to the specialist. But since it\'s "he" instead of "who," it\'s a bit different. Wait, actually, "who" is a relative pronoun, but here it\'s using a personal pronoun "he" in the relative clause. That\'s a bit tricky. \n\nIn such cases, the pronoun "he" is acting as a relative pronoun here, so we have to determine what it\'s referring to. Since the sentence is talking about the patient being referred due to having a condition, the logical antecedent is the patient. The specialist is the one being referred to, but the condition is the patient\'s. So "he" must 